{{!
	Category links footer: the pill-chip treatment of a page's category list.

	One element deep, on purpose. Core has already built this entire region, so the only
	thing this partial adds is a class for the stylesheet to hang the treatment on.

	No literal doubled braces appear anywhere in this comment. A Mustache comment ends at
	the first doubled closing brace it meets, so an inlined tag example would terminate
	the comment early and leak the remainder of it onto the page as text. Tags are
	therefore described in prose below rather than quoted.

	DATA CONSUMED

	string html-categories
		The page's finished category block, produced by Skin::getCategories() and placed
		at the root of the template data by SkinMustache::getTemplateData(). Finished
		HTML, not a list of names. It already contains, and this partial therefore never
		authors:

		  div#catlinks.catlinks      the outer block. Also carries .catlinks-allhidden
		                             when nothing inside it is visible to this user.
		  div#mw-normal-catlinks     core's own label for the region, a link built from
		                             the pagecategories message, then a colon separator,
		                             then a ul holding one li per category.
		  div#mw-hidden-catlinks     present only when the page carries hidden
		                             categories. Also carries exactly one of
		                             .mw-hidden-cats-user-shown,
		                             .mw-hidden-cats-ns-shown or
		                             .mw-hidden-cats-hidden.

	Rebuilding that list from parts is the one mistake this partial exists to prevent.
	Hand-rolling a ul here would silently drop both #mw-hidden-catlinks and core's label
	along with it, and those are selectors rule R2 requires to survive the redesign with
	their semantics unchanged. There is nothing here to iterate and nothing to name: emit
	the string whole and unaltered.

	WHY THE SECTION GUARD, AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY GUARDS

	Wrapping the emission in a section on html-categories is the idiom core-conformant
	skins use for a raw-HTML key that may be absent, the same shape the root template
	uses for html-undelete-link, so a missing value yields no wrapper at all rather than
	an empty one.

	The guard's reach is worth stating exactly, because the obvious reading of it is
	wrong in two places and both are easy to trip over later.

	First, this key is never actually absent. Skin::getCategories() wraps its inner
	content unconditionally on every release the manifest supports, so a page with no
	categories still yields div#catlinks.catlinks.catlinks-allhidden with nothing inside
	it. Only the inner Skin::getCategoryLinks() returns an empty string. The guard is
	therefore defensive rather than load-bearing: it costs one line, and it keeps this
	partial correct if the key is ever null, false, or simply not supplied.

	Second, the guard does not fire on an empty string. MediaWiki compiles Mustache with
	LightnCandy, whose sections treat an empty string, and the string zero, and the
	integer zero, as truthy, and skip only on null, false, an empty array, or an absent
	key. That is exactly why SkinMustache normalises its genuinely optional HTML keys to
	null before handing them over, and why html-categories, never being empty, receives
	no such normalisation.

	The empty-region case is consequently handled where it belongs, in CSS rather than in
	markup: core's interface.category.less gives .catlinks-allhidden a display of none,
	and that rule reaches the page because skin.json enables the interface-category skin
	feature.

	That leaves one constraint on the stylesheet, which is the main reason this note
	exists. components/Footer.less must hang the region's box chrome, meaning its fill,
	border, radius, padding and margin, on the chips and on .catlinks, and never on
	.blitzy-category-footer itself. Core's rule reaches only div#catlinks and says
	nothing about this wrapper, which stays a normal-flow block even when its only child
	generates no box at all. Chrome placed on .catlinks therefore inherits that display
	of none for free and disappears along with the content, whereas chrome placed on the
	wrapper outlives it and paints.

	There are two distinct failure modes here, and both were measured in a browser
	against this exact markup rather than reasoned about. A wrapper carrying background,
	border, radius and padding renders a visibly empty box on every all-hidden page: its
	content box is zero pixels high, so every rendered pixel is padding and border, and
	it announces a region that has no content while contributing nothing to the
	accessibility tree. A wrapper carrying only a margin paints no box but still injects
	an invisible gap. Carrying no chrome at all, which is how it computes today, the
	wrapper collapses to zero height and becomes indistinguishable from not being there.

	WHAT THIS PARTIAL DELIBERATELY DOES NOT GROW

	The wrapper is a plain div carrying exactly one class. Every omission is deliberate:

	  no id            ids must be unique page-wide and this region renders on every
	                   content page, so carrying none is the cheapest guarantee against
	                   a collision, and the cheapest way to emit nothing that could
	                   differ between two identical requests (R9, R10).
	  no nav landmark  core puts no landmark here. Adding one would introduce a region
	                   that then has to be named, for no navigational gain (R10).
	  no heading       div#mw-normal-catlinks already opens with core's pagecategories
	                   link, which is this region's label. A second one would duplicate
	                   it and disturb heading order (R10).
	  no message key   nothing here is worded by this skin, so no key is referenced and
	                   none is added to i18n/en.json, i18n/qqq.json or the manifest's
	                   messages list, where banana-checker would then require it to be
	                   documented (Gate 2).
	  no style attr    every visual decision, meaning the brand-tinted chip fill, the
	                   purple labels and the pill radius, resolves in
	                   components/Footer.less against skins.blitzy.tokens, the only
	                   module permitted a colour literal (R3).
	  no direction     no left-or-right ordering and no directional attribute, so
	                   direction-sensitive rules stay in the flip-aware stylesheet that
	                   core's style pipeline can flip and where noflip is available (R6).
	  no behaviour     no collapse, no counter, no affordance for showing more. The
	                   chips are presentation only (R12).

	Renaming, dropping or wrapping away any core id or class is out of the question for
	the same reason. Footer.less is allowed to style this region directly only because
	the category block is skin output rather than a core ResourceLoader module, and that
	licence holds only while the selectors reaching the page are the ones core produced
	(R7).

	INVOCATION

	ContentBody.mustache includes this file as a bare CategoryFooter partial reference,
	immediately after the body-content key and inside div#bodyContent, at the same
	context level as that key. No parent section pushes a context, so html-categories is
	resolved from the root template data and this file must not assume otherwise.
	Partials resolve by filename, so the exact name CategoryFooter.mustache is part of
	the contract.

	Rendered output, in full: one div.blitzy-category-footer wrapping core's block.
}}
{{#html-categories}}
<div class="blitzy-category-footer">{{{.}}}</div>
{{/html-categories}}
